The attached image (Figure 11) compares the residual inflammation at injection sites two weeks after immunization with aluminum hydroxide (AH) adjuvant (left panel) versus Nano-11 (right panel). The histological sections, stained with H&E, show that AH induced extensive granulomatous inflammation, characterized by widespread inflammatory cells (indicated by asterisks and magnified insets). In stark contrast, Nano-11 resulted in significantly less residual inflammation, with only sparse inflammatory cells arranged in slender cords. This demonstrates that Nano-11 causes a less persistent local inflammatory response compared to aluminum hydroxide, supporting its potential as a safer vaccine adjuvant.